  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure causes a frontal boundary to become semi-\nstationary across the area today. This feature will raise \nchances for shower and/or storm activity through this afternoon\nand possibly the evening. It will also manage to steer a \ncomplex into the area this morning providing rain and possible \nstorm activity. This complex is weak in nature and should not \nimpose any significant threats other than lightning and gusty \nwinds under a downpour. If its track slows down this way we \ncould possibly see some instances of flooding in low lying or \nflood prone areas if training occurs. Cloudy skies and \nseasonable temperatures will keep thunderstorms to the general \nthreat and sub-severe with the lack of shear and upper level \nsupport.\n\nSurface high pressure in place will keep the feature from \ntraversing southward at a fast pace. It will take until Saturday\nmorning to exit the area to the south. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nStarting Saturday, high pressure and subsidence aloft should cap\nus to where a lull from shower or storm activity should exist \nfor the day. As the surface high pressure system pulls back \ntoward the south and weakens this will allow for diurnal \nactivity to take place Sunday afternoon. A frontal boundary is \nthen forecast to impact the area Sunday through Monday with \nincreasing chances for shower and storm activity. The threat is \nstill sub-severe at this point, but excessive rainfall may be \nan aspect, especially if the front becomes semi-stationary \nwhich could promote some flooding instances through its passage.\n\nWPC will have us in a marginal threat for excessive rainfall\nthrough Tuesday. This cold promote instances of isolated flash \nflooding if areas are hit repeatedly with heavy downpours. This\nactive pattern will possibly continue through midweek. \n\nMore chances for activity open up due to a forecast low \npressure system developing over the Great Lakes which will drive\ndown southward toward our area and impact us with more \nunsettled weather. A little too far out to gauge severe \npotential but it will be worth keeping on eye out as this system\nis forecast to impact us through the work week according to \nmedium range models.",
